# Comments (TypeScript / TSX)

## Project preference: minimal comments

- **Default to no new comments.** The maintainers prefer almost no comments; add them only when truly necessary.
- Do **not** add “helpful” narration, section headers, or JSDoc for code that is already clear from names and types.
- If something feels unclear, first try **renaming, extracting a function, or tightening types** before adding a comment.
- When in doubt, **omit the comment**.

## Default

- Prefer **clear names and types** over comments.
- Do **not** add comments that restate what the code already says.

## When an agent may add a comment

Only when omitting it would likely **mislead a maintainer** or hide a **real constraint** (security, protocol shape, unavoidable hack). Limit to **intent, constraints, or tradeoffs** that are not obvious from types or names. Prefer a **single** tagged line (`// NOTE: …`, etc.).

## Allowed markers (prefix with `//`)

| Tag | Use |
|-----|-----|
| `TODO` | Planned work, missing behavior, follow-up |
| `FIXME` | Incorrect or incomplete behavior that should be fixed |
| `HACK` | Temporary workaround; say why if not obvious |
| `NOTE` | Non-obvious contract, data flow, or coupling (e.g. extension ↔ webview) |
| `SECURITY` | Trust boundaries, CSP, secrets, path handling — never log or expose secrets |

**Format:** `// NOTE: …` (one space after the colon is fine.)

## Always preserve (do not remove or “clean up”)

- `/// <reference …>` and other TypeScript file-level directives
- `@ts-expect-error` / `@ts-ignore` and any **required** adjacent explanation
- `eslint-disable` / `eslint-disable-next-line` when needed; keep scope minimal

## Tooling

- ESLint policy is duplicated at the top of `.eslintrc.cjs`; `no-warning-comments` discourages ambiguous `xxx`-style markers.

## Webview / extension boundaries

- Prefer `NOTE` or `SECURITY` for message contracts, CSP, and anything that would be unsafe if mishandled.
